Answer: The main contribution of this paper is to address the issue of zero-shot translation in Neural Machine Translation (NMT) using transfer learning, particularly in scenarios where there is a large amount of parallel data for the pivot language but limited or no parallel data for the target language. The authors argue that the existing transfer learning approaches fail due to the language space mismatch problem, also known as the domain shift problem, which occurs when the source and pivot languages have different feature distributions. To overcome this limitation, the authors propose a novel approach that minimizes the discrepancy between the feature distributions of different source languages, ensuring a smoother transition between the parent and child models. By doing so, they aim to improve the performance of transfer learning in NMT even in zero-resource settings, where only parallel data for the pivot language is available.

